rticipants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/28940"} "24 from 18 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Total of best lifts in military press, snatch, and clean &amp; jerk determined placement.  Ties broken by lightest bodyweight."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "At the 1947 World Championships, American " [:a {:href "/athletes/57023"} "Stan Stanczyk"] " won this class defeating his teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/57022"} "Frank Spellman"] ", while another American, " [:a {:href "/athletes/56977"} "Pete George"] ", won gold as a lightweight. George moved up in weight to compete in London as a middleweight, while Stanczyk also added weight and was in the " [:a {:href "/results/28954"} "light-heavyweight class"] ", leaving George and Spellman as middleweight favorites. The defending champion from " [:a {:href "/results/28856"} "Berlin"] " was Egypt’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/55533"} "Khadr El-Touni"] ", and he was back in an attempt to add another gold medal, and had to be considered, having won the 1946 World Championship."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The lead in the press was taken by Korean " [:a {:href "/athletes/56263"} "Kim Seong-Jip"] ", third at the 1947 Worlds, followed by El-Touni, with Spellman third, and George trailing Kim by 17.5 kg. After the snatch, Spellman and El-Touni were tied at 237.5 kg, followed by Kim, with George moving into fourth place. However, the Americans prevailed in the clean &amp; jerk, with Spellman winning the gold medal when George failed on his final attempt with 165.0 kg. Kim and El-Touni tied with a total of 380.0 kg, but Kim won Korea’s first ever Olympic medal because of his lighter bodyweight (72.92 kg to 74.90 kg)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/57022"} "Frank Spellman"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/co